immediate_aarch64: do not shift a u_int64_t by its own width

replicate() shifts the accumulator left by nbits before or-ing the next
copy in.  expandLogicalImmediate() calls it with nbits = 2 * (1 << i),
which is 64 on the last iteration, so the shift count equals the width
of u_int64_t -- undefined behaviour.  ones() already guards the same
case for the mask; the shift never got the treatment.

With the android Zero build this never showed up because cpu/aarch64 is
not compiled there.  In the server build the optimiser is free to
rewrite the loop, a batch of the 8192 entries in InverseLITable comes
out wrong, qsort puts them in the wrong place, and bsearch then fails to
find encodings that are actually representable -- encode_logical_immediate()
returns 0xffffffff and Assembler::andr() dies on

  guarantee(val < (1U << nbits)) failed: Field too big for insn

in StubGenerator::generate_call_stub().  Compiled at -O0 the hardware
shift makes the loop a no-op and the table is correct, which is what
pinned this to the optimiser.

Skip the shift when it would be by the full width. nbits == 64 is only
reached with count == 1, so the result is unchanged and the undefined
behaviour is gone.

diff --git a/hotspot/src/cpu/aarch64/vm/immediate_aarch64.cpp b/hotspot/src/cpu/aarch64/vm/immediate_aarch64.cpp
index 9f606ff..2fb2463 100644
--- a/hotspot/src/cpu/aarch64/vm/immediate_aarch64.cpp
+++ b/hotspot/src/cpu/aarch64/vm/immediate_aarch64.cpp
@@ -126,7 +126,11 @@ u_int64_t replicate(u_int64_t bits, int nbits, int count)
   // nbits may be 64 in which case we want mask to be -1
   u_int64_t mask = ones(nbits);
   for (int i = 0; i < count ; i++) {
-    result <<= nbits;
+    // nbits is 64 when replicating a 64-bit element; shifting by the width
+    // of the type is undefined and lets the optimiser rewrite this loop.
+    if (nbits < 64) {
+      result <<= nbits;
+    }
     result |= (bits & mask);
   }
   return result;
